During a regularly scheduled defrag of our Digital Unix 4.0d system's AdvFS
partitions, a bad sector was found.  We renamed the file, then recovered
what we could from it and rebuilt the database it contained into a new file
with the old filename, so our system is up and all is well.

However, every night I get this error message in my defrag results:

defragment: Defragmented domain 'root_domain'
defragment: Can't move file /dlc/database/inv/baditr.datafile
defragment: Error = I/O error
defragment: Can't defragment domain 'db_domain'
defragcron: could not defragment domain 'db_domain'

It's doing no harm I can see (and, after a few weeks, no more bad sectors
have cropped up, so it doesn't seem that this is more than an isolated
failure on the disk).  However, I've been unable to figure out how to get
rid of it.  I can't delete the bad data file and wouldn't want to if I
could, as I don't want that sector used by anything else.  I've tried
dismounting and running verify but it reported no problems and made no
changes.

Any suggestions?

